      "content": "Once the configuration is ready, GridDeploy may require manual authentication depending on your organization's security policies, typically accessed via the `gd auth` subcommand or by loading your AWS profile directly. You can run the `gd deploy` command for any environment by specifying both the target environment and the path to your configuration file in a single operation to trigger the standard build and push workflow.\n\n### Prerequisites\nBefore attempting any deployment, ensure three critical components are in place. You must have AWS credentials configured in `~/.aws/credentials` because GridDeploy relies on IAM roles to push container images to your private registry and execute EC2 IAM actions. Docker must be running locally as the CLI instruments image builds and layer analysis directly within your container instance. Finally, a `grid.yaml` is mandatory as it serves as the source of truth for version tagging, instance types, and region mapping throughout the entire lifecycle of your application.\n\n### YAML Configuration Breakdown\nYour `grid.yaml` file is structured to map your environment needs to cloud resources. The `environments` block defines specific settings like the AWS region and the number of replicas, ensuring consistent scaling across your pipeline. Within `versions`, the `registry` specifies your private container host while the `",
